right well it's now been one week since  russian energy company gas prompt  drastically reduced gas deliveries  through its north stream pipeline to  germany to just 20 percent of its normal  flow russia says maintenance issues and  western sanctions are preventing it from  pumping more gas but germany's  chancellor olaf schultz today accused  russia of blocking the delivery of a  turbine destined for the pipeline the  turbine is now in germany after being  repaired in canada  inspecting the giant turbine schultz  said it was working and ready to use  once russia requested it  schultz said russia's claims of  technical problems were not credible and  said gas customers should expect more  restrictions  we need to understand that it is not  enough to do everything right  in this difficult situation related to  russia's war in ukraine  we must be clear that there can always  be a pretext that leads to something not  working  technical reasons have allegedly also  impacted gas exports to other european  countries  there too they were also  incomprehensible  all right well for more on this let's  bring in dw's political correspondent  emmanuel shaz manuel good to see you  should we consider chancellor schultz's  visit to this turbine  a big statement from him  it is a big statement and above all  pablo it shows you just how important  this matter is not only at a german  level but  the international dimension of this you  know this turbine being put back in  service or not this turbine in fact is  working just fine there's no reason why  it shouldn't be transported that was the  message from the chancellor so that was  quite clear and the delays don't come  from the german side that's what olasha  said that there's no government decision  that makes the transport of this turbine  impossible nor is there any technical  issue on siemens's side no technical  reasons uh whatsoever that is what allah  shows said so the chancellor does blame  russia for any delay incurring and he  insisted that the truth and facts were  important at a moment where we witnessed  a war that triggered this energy crisis  but a war that is not just happening on  the ukrainian front line but it is that  is also a very much a global economic  conflict at this stage  now the chancellor also announced a  series of measures that deal directly  with the energy crisis what more can you  tell us on those  he did indeed aside from what we already  knew for example the efforts that must  be made by industries companies and the  population to save up as much energy as  possible before the winter all of  schultz also mentioned the importance of  filling up the gas reserves  and announce more aid packages for those  who need it the most for example mostly  financial support for low income  families he talked about 30  billion euros that will be used to that  effect  thank you emmanuel dw's political  correspondent emmanuel shaz
 

